Add be-cpp's to_base, an upcast of a shared_ptr that's never null, so g++'s -Wnull-dereference has no null path to follow.
Upstream: not yet; logged for a Kotlin Temper fix (Kotlin's C++ trips -Wnull-dereference on g++ 12 to 14 at -O1 and up).

Interfaces are virtual bases, so converting a shared_ptr<Class> to
shared_ptr<Interface> reads the object's vtable for the offset unless the
pointer is null, and gives null if it is. That null path reaches the
next `->` on the result, which g++ reports as a potential null
dereference (and isolates, with a trap), though Temper's types say the
value is never null. to_base<Target>(src) tells the compiler so
(__builtin_unreachable, or MSVC's __assume) before converting, which
drops the check, the path, and the warning; the backend emits it for an
upcast of a value that isn't nullable.

diff --git a/be-cpp/temper-core/casting.hpp b/be-cpp/temper-core/casting.hpp
--- a/be-cpp/temper-core/casting.hpp
+++ b/be-cpp/temper-core/casting.hpp
@@ -1,5 +1,6 @@
 #pragma once
 #include <memory>
